\section*{AP \({ }^{\text {® }}\) CALCULUS BC \\ 2011 SCORING GUIDELINES}

\section*{Question 3}

Let \(f(x)=e^{2 x}\). Let \(R\) be the region in the first quadrant bounded by the graph of \(f\), the coordinate axes, and the vertical line \(x=k\), where \(k>0\). The region \(R\) is shown in the figure above.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Write, but do not evaluate, an expression involving an integral that gives the perimeter of \(R\) in terms of \(k\).
\item[(b)] The region \(R\) is rotated about the \(x\)-axis to form a solid. Find the volume, \(V\), of the solid in terms of \(k\).
\end{itemize}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/1078bbe3-b6be-4f25-b56c-c75f6b4de2e9-1.jpg?height=440&width=593&top_left_y=400&top_left_x=1349)
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] The volume \(V\), found in part (b), changes as \(k\) changes. If \(\frac{d k}{d t}=\frac{1}{3}\), determine \(\frac{d V}{d t}\) when \(k=\frac{1}{2}\).
\item[(a)] \(f^{\prime}(x)=2 e^{2 x}\)
\end{itemize}
\[
\text { Perimeter }=1+k+e^{2 k}+\int_{0}^{k} \sqrt{1+\left(2 e^{2 x}\right)^{2}} d x
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(b)] Volume \(=\pi \int_{0}^{k}\left(e^{2 x}\right)^{2} d x=\pi \int_{0}^{k} e^{4 x} d x=\left.\frac{\pi}{4} e^{4 x}\right|_{x=0} ^{x=k}=\frac{\pi}{4} e^{4 k}-\frac{\pi}{4}\)
\item[(c)] \(\frac{d V}{d t}=\pi e^{4 k} \frac{d k}{d t}\)
When \(k=\frac{1}{2}, \frac{d V}{d t}=\pi e^{2} \cdot \frac{1}{3}\).
\end{itemize}
\(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: f^{\prime}(x) \\ 1: \text { integral } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
